00:06:13.455 --> 00:06:16.665
And there I do a lot of
listening to the mini stories.

00:06:16.665 --> 00:06:17.775
I've mentioned them before.

00:06:17.775 --> 00:06:19.095
There's a lot of repetition.

00:06:19.125 --> 00:06:21.225
I find it's good exercise.

00:06:21.225 --> 00:06:24.135
I can listen to them now
for the 30th or 40th time.

00:06:24.135 --> 00:06:24.945
It doesn't matter.

00:06:25.095 --> 00:06:26.415
I always notice something.

00:06:26.625 --> 00:06:28.245
That's kind of my, my training.

00:06:28.245 --> 00:06:32.135
That's the equivalent of my
exercise in the morning, but I also

00:06:32.135 --> 00:06:33.695
need to find interesting things.

00:06:35.609 --> 00:06:38.390
You know, I spend a lot of time
looking for them, the content,

00:06:38.390 --> 00:06:40.010
especially in Arabic and Persian.

00:06:40.340 --> 00:06:44.359
Um, we are trying in LingQ to
make it easier for people to find

00:06:44.390 --> 00:06:46.369
things of interest at their level.

00:06:46.700 --> 00:06:48.109
But sometimes you have to ask.

00:06:48.109 --> 00:06:52.159
I saw a tweet the other day, someone
said, does anyone know of any good

00:06:52.159 --> 00:06:54.190
intermediate level French podcasts?

00:06:54.190 --> 00:06:55.729
So I immediately replied.

00:06:55.729 --> 00:06:59.525
I said, there's  Français Authentique
and there's innerFrench that I

00:06:59.615 --> 00:07:01.175
am, that immediately come to mind.

00:07:01.175 --> 00:07:02.315
So I answered.

00:07:02.435 --> 00:07:06.065
So right away, this person can go
there and find these podcasts, which

00:07:06.065 --> 00:07:07.865
can be imported into LingQ by the way.

00:07:08.285 --> 00:07:10.835
But there's a whole community
of people who can help each

00:07:10.835 --> 00:07:13.235
other find content of interest.

00:07:13.255 --> 00:07:19.585
So, you know, and this, the great thing
about listening and why listening, it

00:07:19.585 --> 00:07:24.885
kind of helps you find the time, is I,
it's more difficult to find the time to

00:07:24.885 --> 00:07:31.055
sit in front of a, of a screen and, and
read or to watch movies or to look or

00:07:31.055 --> 00:07:33.365
to do LingQ, that that's dedicated time.

00:07:33.365 --> 00:07:37.775
You have to, that's more difficult to
find, but the listening time you make

00:07:37.775 --> 00:07:42.575
that, and that triggers the whole a range
of activities around language learning.

00:07:42.875 --> 00:07:45.515
And that can be done first thing in
the morning while doing the dishes.

00:07:46.380 --> 00:07:50.550
Um, you know, while in the car
and I'll find that, uh, certain

00:07:50.550 --> 00:07:53.250
activities that lend themselves
to certain types of content.

00:07:53.460 --> 00:07:57.120
If I have to concentrate a little bit
on what I'm doing, then I prefer to hear

00:07:57.120 --> 00:08:00.840
the mini stories, because even if I don't
concentrate the whole time, there's always

00:08:00.840 --> 00:08:05.190
something that I become aware of, that
I notice that I hadn't noticed before.

00:08:05.670 --> 00:08:09.810
Uh, on the other hand, if I'm in
the car, actually I find it easier

00:08:09.810 --> 00:08:11.160
to listen to something of interest.

00:08:11.200 --> 00:08:14.250
So it's a bit like if you're listening
to the radio and they're giving you the

00:08:14.250 --> 00:08:17.220
traffic report or something, you can
actually listen to something where they,

00:08:17.560 --> 00:08:22.860
there is some significance to what you're
listening to and at the same time drive

00:08:23.360 --> 00:08:24.870
a little more difficult to do that.

00:08:25.260 --> 00:08:28.680
If I'm say working out in the morning,
for example, and I'm concentrating on

00:08:28.680 --> 00:08:32.460
whatever I'm doing, then I prefer to have
the mini stories going in the background.

00:08:33.360 --> 00:08:35.880
So I think everyone needs to
develop a program for themselves.

00:08:36.750 --> 00:08:41.339
But I just wanted to point out that,
that to find the time to make the time to

00:08:41.339 --> 00:08:43.529
learn languages, listening is the easy...

00:08:43.869 --> 00:08:45.209
is the easiest activity.

00:08:45.420 --> 00:08:51.959
And you need to build a range of sort of,
uh, circumstances around what devices you

00:08:51.959 --> 00:08:56.579
use to play whatever you're listening to,
where you find the stuff you're going to

00:08:56.579 --> 00:09:02.295
listen to varying the sort of repetitive,
simple stuff, more interesting new stuff,

00:09:02.655 --> 00:09:07.605
uh, different, you know, ear phones that
you can use for different circumstances.

00:09:07.995 --> 00:09:12.915
And, you know, put yourself in a situation
where you can easily listen, anytime you

00:09:12.915 --> 00:09:17.564
want to, or feel the urge, or even if
you don't feel the urge so that you make

00:09:17.564 --> 00:09:22.845
that first step that eventually will lead
you to putting in the time necessary to

00:09:23.085 --> 00:09:25.275
achieve success in language learning.

00:09:25.635 --> 00:09:30.285
So with that, I'll leave you with a few
videos that kind of relate to this theme.

00:09:30.795 --> 00:09:31.635
